  "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2033}-4y} & =L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{\u2033}}-4L{y} & =L{{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L{y}=Y(s).$ \n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{\u2033}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{\u2033}}={s}^{2}L{y}-sy(0)-{y}^{\u2032}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{\u2032}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{\u2032}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-1.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{1}{s+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2033}}-4L{y} & =L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-1)-4Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1}+1 \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{s+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{(s+1)({s}^{2}-4)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{(s+1)(s-2)(s+2)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
